schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Treasury Management for DAOs"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    Practical guide to DAO treasury management: the "native token problem"
    (thin liquidity, extreme volatility, broken operational continuity when
    a treasury holds mostly its own governance token), the case for holding
    2-3 years of operating expenses in stablecoins, four diversification
    methods (earning stablecoin revenue, strategic token sales, strategic
    partnerships, borrowing against tokens), and Maya Protocol as a
    transitioning-to-DAO-governance example.
  audience: "🌱 Maya (beginner track, DAO governance/finance foundations)"
  known_tensions:
    - "diversification improves resilience but strategic token sales and borrowing both carry market-impact / liquidation risk"
    - "treasury management choices are framed as governance choices (who holds power), not purely financial ones"
  related_concepts:
    - dao-treasury-diversification
    - native-token-concentration-risk
  related_resources:
    - Maya Protocol
    - Aave
    - Gnosis Auction
  high_risk: true
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/treasury-management.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
# - No 8-forms-of-capital framing appears anywhere in this article — it is
#   purely financial/crypto-treasury content (stablecoins, tokens, lending
#   protocols). No capital-forms claim-evidence/signal objects were created
#   since there is no such language to capture faithfully.
# - Wintermute, UMA Protocol "success tokens", Compound, Element, Yield:
#   named only in passing as one-line examples of OTC/borrowing mechanisms —
#   not given standalone resource objects to avoid inflating minor mentions
#   into first-class entries; noted here for traceability instead.
# - The article itself carries `sources: []` in its own frontmatter despite
#   citing precise figures ($15B, $748M, 95% slippage) — flagged separately
#   as a signal (03-signal.yaml) rather than silently treated as verified fact.
